1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,873
A tidy room gets messy all by itself.

2
00:00:02,873 --> 00:00:05,389
A drop of perfume fills the whole room.

3
00:00:05,389 --> 00:00:07,088
Ice melts on a warm day.

4
00:00:07,489 --> 00:00:08,784
Nobody pushes these.

5
00:00:08,784 --> 00:00:09,943
They just happen.

6
00:00:09,943 --> 00:00:11,754
But they never run backwards.

7
00:00:12,204 --> 00:00:13,723
Diya studies at night.

8
00:00:13,723 --> 00:00:15,643
Her desk was tidy on Monday.

9
00:00:15,643 --> 00:00:17,066
By Friday, a mess.

10
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:18,913
It never tidies itself.

11
00:00:18,913 --> 00:00:21,066
Why does nature pick one direction?

12
00:00:21,566 --> 00:00:24,017
By the end, you will know
the hidden rule that decides.

13
00:00:24,667 --> 00:00:29,857
A change that happens on its own, without
a constant push, is called spontaneous.

14
00:00:30,207 --> 00:00:32,561
Spontaneous does not mean fast.

15
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:35,329
Iron rusts on its own, but slowly.

16
00:00:35,729 --> 00:00:37,056
So what decides it?

17
00:00:37,056 --> 00:00:38,521
Maybe giving out heat?

18
00:00:38,521 --> 00:00:41,175
A ball rolls downhill
and gives out energy.

19
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:45,501
But ice melting takes heat in,
and still happens on its own.

20
00:00:45,501 --> 00:00:47,739
So heat alone cannot be the rule.

21
00:00:48,339 --> 00:00:50,932
Let's look closer on our molecule bench.

22
00:00:50,932 --> 00:00:52,872
Two flasks joined by a tap.

23
00:00:52,872 --> 00:00:55,069
All the gas starts on the left.

24
00:00:55,419 --> 00:00:56,319
Open the tap.

25
00:00:56,272 --> 00:00:58,781
The molecules wander and
spread into both flasks.

26
00:00:59,181 --> 00:01:00,620
No energy pushed them.

27
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:04,448
There are simply far more ways
to be spread out than bunched up.

28
00:01:04,848 --> 00:01:07,118
They never all crowd back to one side.

29
00:01:07,118 --> 00:01:09,423
Just like your room never tidies itself.

30
00:01:10,023 --> 00:01:13,925
This spread is measured by entropy:
the amount of disorder, the number

31
00:01:13,925 --> 00:01:15,811
of ways things can be arranged.

32
00:01:16,161 --> 00:01:18,282
A tidy room has few arrangements.

33
00:01:18,282 --> 00:01:19,830
A messy room has many.

34
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:21,463
Messy means high entropy.

35
00:01:21,863 --> 00:01:22,763
Now matter.

36
00:01:22,717 --> 00:01:25,343
In a solid, particles sit in fixed rows.

37
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:26,244
Low entropy.

38
00:01:26,644 --> 00:01:29,356
In a liquid, they slide past each other.

39
00:01:29,356 --> 00:01:30,334
More entropy.

40
00:01:30,734 --> 00:01:32,922
In a gas, they fly everywhere.

41
00:01:32,922 --> 00:01:34,338
The highest entropy.

42
00:01:34,338 --> 00:01:36,516
Solid, then liquid, then gas.

43
00:01:37,116 --> 00:01:39,686
How do we measure a change in entropy?

44
00:01:39,686 --> 00:01:40,741
Step by step.

45
00:01:41,091 --> 00:01:41,991
Step one.

46
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:44,677
Add a little heat, q, gently and slowly.

47
00:01:44,677 --> 00:01:46,389
This is called reversibly.

48
00:01:47,139 --> 00:01:48,039
Step two.

49
00:01:48,034 --> 00:01:50,841
Divide by the temperature T in kelvin.

50
00:01:50,841 --> 00:01:53,483
Delta S equals q reversible over T.

51
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:55,158
Step three.

52
00:01:55,158 --> 00:01:56,455
Why divide by T?

53
00:01:56,455 --> 00:01:59,623
A sneeze in a quiet
library causes a big stir.

54
00:01:59,623 --> 00:02:01,795
On a busy street, hardly any.

55
00:02:02,545 --> 00:02:06,429
Heat added to something cold makes
more new disorder than the same heat

56
00:02:06,429 --> 00:02:07,884
added to something hot.

57
00:02:08,634 --> 00:02:11,342
The unit is joules per kelvin per mole.

58
00:02:12,092 --> 00:02:13,329
Now the big rule.

59
00:02:13,329 --> 00:02:16,116
Count the system and its
surroundings together.

60
00:02:16,466 --> 00:02:20,226
For any spontaneous change,
the total entropy goes up.

61
00:02:20,226 --> 00:02:22,760
Delta S total is greater than zero.

62
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,425
This is the idea of the
second law of thermodynamics.

63
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:29,793
At equilibrium, the total does not change.

64
00:02:29,793 --> 00:02:31,499
Delta S total is zero.

65
00:02:31,899 --> 00:02:33,329
The surroundings matter.

66
00:02:33,329 --> 00:02:36,751
When a system gives out heat,
the surroundings get messier.

67
00:02:37,401 --> 00:02:39,607
Compare two changes side by side.

68
00:02:39,607 --> 00:02:41,363
Ice melts at ten degrees.

69
00:02:41,363 --> 00:02:43,217
Water freezes at minus ten.

70
00:02:43,617 --> 00:02:45,551
Melting makes the water messier.

71
00:02:45,551 --> 00:02:47,117
Freezing makes it tidier.

72
00:02:47,117 --> 00:02:48,970
Yet both happen on their own.

73
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:50,469
Pause and think.

74
00:02:50,469 --> 00:02:52,525
Freezing lowers the water's entropy.

75
00:02:52,525 --> 00:02:54,500
How can it still be spontaneous?

76
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:01,004
The freezing water gives heat
to the cold surroundings.

77
00:03:01,004 --> 00:03:03,879
Their entropy rises more
than the water's falls.

78
00:03:03,879 --> 00:03:05,176
The total goes up.

79
00:03:05,826 --> 00:03:06,936
Remember this.

80
00:03:06,936 --> 00:03:11,406
For a spontaneous change, the total
entropy of system and surroundings

81
00:03:11,406 --> 00:03:12,306
increases.

82
00:03:12,946 --> 00:03:14,636
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:14,936 --> 00:03:17,301
Iron rusts at two ninety eight kelvin.

84
00:03:17,301 --> 00:03:21,183
The system's entropy change is minus
five forty nine point four joules

85
00:03:21,183 --> 00:03:22,553
per kelvin per mole.

86
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:26,040
The enthalpy change is minus
sixteen forty eight kilojoules.

87
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:30,315
The surroundings receive sixteen
forty eight thousand joules of heat.

88
00:03:31,065 --> 00:03:35,543
Their entropy change is sixteen forty
eight thousand divided by two ninety

89
00:03:35,543 --> 00:03:36,443
eight.

90
00:03:36,154 --> 00:03:37,848
About fifty five thirty.

91
00:03:38,598 --> 00:03:39,498
Total.

92
00:03:39,197 --> 00:03:42,625
Fifty five thirty, minus
five forty nine point four.

93
00:03:42,625 --> 00:03:45,748
Plus four thousand nine
hundred eighty point six.

94
00:03:46,498 --> 00:03:47,925
The total is positive.

95
00:03:47,925 --> 00:03:51,927
So rusting is spontaneous, even
though the iron becomes more ordered.

96
00:03:52,577 --> 00:03:54,039
Now, the exam trap.

97
00:03:54,389 --> 00:03:58,657
Students say: the system's entropy went
down, so the change cannot be spontaneous.

98
00:03:59,057 --> 00:03:59,957
Wrong.

99
00:03:59,671 --> 00:04:02,909
Always check the total,
system plus surroundings.

100
00:04:03,559 --> 00:04:05,692
ISC students, one more rule.

101
00:04:05,692 --> 00:04:06,754
The third law.

102
00:04:06,754 --> 00:04:09,939
A perfect crystal at absolute
zero has zero entropy.

103
00:04:10,539 --> 00:04:12,011
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:12,011 --> 00:04:14,144
Boards ask the second law idea.

105
00:04:14,144 --> 00:04:18,263
Entrance tests ask you to predict
the sign of an entropy change.

106
00:04:18,863 --> 00:04:20,117
Exam level now.

107
00:04:20,117 --> 00:04:23,116
Which change has a
positive entropy change?

108
00:04:23,116 --> 00:04:24,636
A, water freezing.

109
00:04:24,636 --> 00:04:27,846
B, making ammonia from
nitrogen and hydrogen.

110
00:04:27,846 --> 00:04:31,493
C, calcium carbonate giving
lime and carbon dioxide.

111
00:04:31,493 --> 00:04:33,555
D, making sulphur trioxide.

112
00:04:33,555 --> 00:04:34,863
Pause and solve.

113
00:04:40,263 --> 00:04:41,606
The answer is C.

114
00:04:42,106 --> 00:04:43,648
Count the gas molecules.

115
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:45,291
Gas has the most entropy.

116
00:04:45,791 --> 00:04:47,937
In C, a solid makes a gas.

117
00:04:47,937 --> 00:04:49,804
Zero gas becomes one gas.

118
00:04:49,804 --> 00:04:50,913
Entropy rises.

119
00:04:51,413 --> 00:04:53,592
In B and D, gas molecules decrease.

120
00:04:53,592 --> 00:04:55,321
In A, liquid becomes solid.

121
00:04:55,321 --> 00:04:56,341
All three fall.

122
00:04:56,941 --> 00:04:58,482
Here is your recap.

123
00:04:58,482 --> 00:05:00,067
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:03,267 --> 00:05:05,452
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:05,852 --> 00:05:06,752
One.

126
00:05:06,353 --> 00:05:08,161
Does spontaneous mean fast?

127
00:05:08,961 --> 00:05:09,861
Two.

128
00:05:09,463 --> 00:05:12,600
Put solid, liquid and
gas in order of entropy.

129
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:14,300
Three.

130
00:05:13,911 --> 00:05:17,653
How can a change that lowers the
system's entropy still be spontaneous?

131
00:05:18,553 --> 00:05:22,977
Next time: one number that decides
direction, without counting the surroundings?

132
00:05:22,977 --> 00:05:24,717
We find out in Gibbs energy.
